  "hero.title": "How an Encrypted Local Image Gets Decoded",
  "hero.kicker": "Computing · Systems & Security · An Interactive Essay",
  "hero.sub": "The full image sits right there on disk, yet renaming it to `.jpg` refuses to open.<br>Starting from raw bytes, how do you deduce its encryption layout and locate the real key?",
  "hero.lead": "Many desktop clients encrypt cached chat images into local `.dat` files. Without source code or documentation—only the encrypted files on disk and a running client—how can you reliably restore them into standard images? This essay walks through a real-world debugging path: <strong>deducing container layout and block modes directly from ciphertext bytes, understanding why process memory scanning misses a 1-millisecond key lifetime, and finally using ARM64 PC-relative addressing and C++ runtime memory layout to capture the key cleanly at a single function return.</strong>",
  "hero.note": "Everything here is fully redacted with no personal keys, accounts, or private paths. Each chapter focuses on a general systems or cryptography principle with a hands-on model. Written by Antigravity (Gemini 4 Neon) from a real debugging session; every number in the prose is computed by the page's model scripts.",
  "ch1.title": "Why Renaming Fails: Hybrid Encrypted Containers",
  "toc.ch1": "Hybrid Encrypted Containers",
  "ch1.law": "Modern clients rarely encrypt an entire image with an expensive block cipher; instead they lock the file header with AES and mask the rest with a lightweight byte operation.",
  "ch1.p1": "Inspecting a V2 local `.dat` image reveals a consistent 15-byte header: a 6-byte magic signature `07 08 56 32 08 07` (embedding ASCII `V2`), two 4-byte little-endian unsigned integers giving the <strong>AES plaintext length</strong> (typically `1024` bytes) and the <strong>XOR tail length</strong>, plus a 1-byte flag.",
  "ch1.p2": "Comparing a `99,493`-byte plaintext PNG with its corresponding `.dat` shows the encrypted file is `99,524` bytes—an overhead of exactly `31` bytes. That gap is not random: 15 bytes come from the container header, and 16 bytes come from <strong>PKCS#7 padding</strong>. When the plaintext length (`1024`) is already a multiple of the 16-byte AES block size, PKCS#7 appends a full extra block of sixteen `0x10` bytes, making the AES ciphertext segment `1040` bytes long while the remaining `98,469` bytes use single-byte XOR.",
  "dg.layout.cap": "Fig 1. Four-segment layout of a V2 hybrid encrypted container: 15-byte header, AES ciphertext of the first 1024 bytes, a 16-byte PKCS#7 padding block, and the XOR-masked tail.",
