  "ch1.say": "Out of {plain} plaintext bytes, the first {aes} bytes plus {pad} bytes of PKCS#7 padding become <em>{aesPad} bytes</em> of AES ciphertext (offsets 15..{aesEnd}); the remaining {xor} bytes are XOR-masked, adding <em>{diff} bytes</em> in total.",
  "ch1.limit": "Locking the first 1024 bytes—which hold the image magic number, dimensions, color tables, and Huffman tables—prevents any standard viewer from opening the file while keeping encryption overhead near zero on multi-megabyte images.",
  "ch2.title": "XOR Involution: Recovering the Tail Key in Two Bytes",
  "toc.ch2": "XOR Involution",
  "ch2.law": "Single-byte XOR is its own inverse: applying it twice cancels out. Any known fixed trailer bytes in an image format reveal the XOR key immediately.",
  "ch2.p1": "Exclusive-OR (`⊕`) obeys two simple identities: any byte XORed with itself is `0` ($K \\oplus K = 0$), and any byte XORed with `0` is unchanged ($P \\oplus 0 = P$). If encryption computes $C = P \\oplus K$, then XORing the ciphertext byte with its known plaintext immediately isolates the key: $C \\oplus P = (P \\oplus K) \\oplus P = K$.",
  "ch2.p2": "Standard image formats end with fixed signatures: JPEG always finishes with `FF D9`, and PNG always ends with the `IEND` chunk `49 45 4E 44 AE 42 60 82`. XORing the last two bytes of several thumbnail `.dat` files with `FF` and `D9` yields the exact same byte on both positions—`0x19`—and checking all 98 KB of a known PNG tail confirms every byte shares that single key.",
  "dg.xor.cap": "Fig 2. XORing the fixed JPEG trailer FF D9 with the last two bytes of the .dat file immediately yields the single-byte key 0x19.",
  "dg.xor.aria": "Diagram: recovering XOR key 0x19 from known JPEG trailer FF D9 and ciphertext E6 C0",
