  "ch3.say.ecb": "Under <em>ECB</em>, even though Image A and Image B differ, their trailing padding blocks share the same plaintext and produce identical ciphertext—a dead giveaway for ECB.",
  "ch3.say.cbc": "Under <em>CBC</em>, differences in earlier blocks chain forward through XOR, making the trailing padding ciphertexts completely unrelated.",
  "ch3.limit": "Security guides warn against ECB on structured files precisely because repeating plaintext blocks leak directly into repeating ciphertext blocks.",
  "ch4.title": "Known-Plaintext Verification: Pushing False Positives to 2⁻¹²⁸",
  "toc.ch4": "Known-Plaintext Verification",
  "ch4.law": "Having both a plaintext image and its ciphertext cannot algebraically invert AES, but it gives you a zero-assumption, zero-false-positive oracle for testing candidate keys.",
  "ch4.p1": "Opening one image via the client's \"Open With Preview\" writes the decrypted plaintext file to a temporary folder. Pairing that file with its `.dat` gives an exact 16-byte plaintext block `PT[0:16]` (such as a PNG header `89 50 4E 47 ...`) and its matching 16-byte ciphertext `CT[15:31]`.",
  "ch4.p2": "AES is built to resist <strong>known-plaintext attacks</strong>—even thousands of `(PT, CT)` pairs will not let you solve for `K`. Yet as a verification oracle during memory scanning or instrumentation, an exact pair is decisive:",
  "ch4.o1": "Checking only a 3-byte JPEG magic number `FF D8 FF` (24 bits) across 550 million candidate keys is expected to trigger <strong>32.8 false positives</strong>;",
  "ch4.o2": "Assuming the 65th block must decrypt to `0x10 × 16` risks rejecting the true key if the client uses zero padding;",
  "ch4.o3": "Checking full 16-byte equality (128 bits) across 550 million candidates has an expected false-positive count around <strong>10<sup>-30</sup></strong>—effectively zero—with no padding assumptions at all.",
  "ch4.model.title": "Verification Bit-Width and False-Positive Rate",
  "ch4.model.how": "Switch between three verification strategies and drag the number of scanned candidate keys (in millions) to compare expected false positives and padding-assumption risk.",
