  "ch5.title": "Why Memory Scanning Misses a 1-Millisecond Window",
  "toc.ch5": "Ephemeral Memory Windows",
  "ch5.law": "When a key lives in RAM for less than a millisecond during image decoding while a memory sweep takes seconds, polling is statistically bound to miss it.",
  "ch5.p1": "On macOS, once a binary's restricted signature is removed, a privileged scanner can call Mach kernel APIs—`task_for_pid`, `mach_vm_region`, and `mach_vm_read_overwrite`—to iterate over every readable virtual memory region and inspect its bytes. This works reliably for <strong>long-lived</strong> database keys that stay resident in heap structures.",
  "ch5.p2": "Yet scanning over 2.3 GB of memory and testing 550 million candidates found nothing, and continuous watch-mode polling missed too. The arithmetic explains why: scanning 1.35 GB takes roughly <strong>3.0 s</strong>, while decoding a 100 KB image takes under <strong>1 ms</strong>, after which the temporary buffer is freed or overwritten. A single random pass overlaps that 1 ms window with probability <strong>0.033%</strong>; even 30 back-to-back passes only reach <strong>1.00%</strong>.",
  "dg.window.cap": "Fig 4. A three-order-of-magnitude mismatch: one memory scan pass takes ~3,000 ms, while the key buffer exists for under 1 ms.",
  "dg.window.aria": "Diagram: comparing a 3000 ms memory scan sweep against a 1 ms key residency window",
  "dg.window.scan": "One full memory scan pass",
  "dg.window.key": "Key residency window in RAM",
  "dg.window.pulse": "< 1 ms (freed right after decode)",
  "ch5.model.title": "Simulate Polling Hit Probability",
  "ch5.model.how": "Adjust scanned process memory, ephemeral key lifetime, and polling rounds to see how cumulative hit probability behaves.",
  "ch5.ctl.ram": "Scanned memory",
  "ch5.ctl.life": "Key lifetime in RAM",
  "ch5.ctl.rounds": "Polling rounds",
  "ch5.cv.aria": "Chart: cumulative probability of overlapping an ephemeral key window across polling rounds",
  "ch5.read.scan": "Time per scan pass",
